A||N||5||darkmagic||brake pedal travel? adjustable?||||1||
Z||000000||darkmagic||08-16-2001||09:11 PM||||Does anyone know if there is a way to adjust the travel of the brake pedal before the brakes bite?<P>I just had the rear calipers and pad replaced, as well as the master cylinder but the brake travel is still pretty far.. any ideas?<P>the e-brake seems to work better now that things are changed in the back.. but the travel is still far. <P>Also... the brakes work well, but there is no pressure on the brake pedal when I press it down.. then all of sudden when pressing the brakes they will bite hard and the wheels will lock.. any ideas on why there is no pressure? brake booster bad? <P>HELP<P>TIA||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||08-16-2001||10:50 PM||||one cause is the booster out of adjustment but that is only likely if you repalced the booster.<P>more likely you've got excessive clearance in the rear calipers. maybe the fronts too if a wheel bearing is sloppy.||||reg||1
Z||000002||darkmagic||08-17-2001||07:16 AM||||how do you make an adjustment on the booster? Is there a manual way to change the travel of the pedal?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Indiana_resto_guy||08-17-2001||07:52 AM||||The only 'pedal adjustment' that can be made is to get your system working properly. Did you bleed the master cylinder on the bench prior to installation?<BR>Were the brakes bled properly? ( RR, LR, RF then LF)<BR>Are all the bleeder valves tight?<BR>When you refer to the e-brake, in what context is travel too far? Still the pedal or the e-brake handle?<BR>Which wheels lock up? A bad booster typically results in a vacuum leak either inside or outside and you will hear a hiss in the passenger compartment or in the trunk area. Due to the configuration there may be a leak in a fitting or hose from front to back as well.<P>theogre:<BR>Booster adjustment? That's a new one, enlighten me or if it's in the cave LMK and I can research it on my own.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||08-17-2001||08:27 PM||||Dark,<P>I very much doubt you need to adjust the booster. Excessive Caliper clearance or a bad MC is more likely your problem. In your case since you just replaced the rears, I'm betting on the calipers. This is a real common issue with them. My own rear calipers have been doing this since the last pad replacement but the weather here has been to screwy to mess with them.<P>Read the brake service article in my cave.<P>I should have an update to that article explaining how to manually force the calipers to the correct clearance sometime next week. GM released a TSB about doing this but I want to actually try it before I try to explain it. The TSB is a little hard to follow if you've never read GM literature before. I also have to make the required shim for the procedure...<P>Indy,<P>The "plunger" in the booster that pushes the MC can be out of adjustment. This is only likely to happen on a booster that has been rebuilt incorrectly. No one would normally fool with that. If plunger is too short you get long pedal travel. If it's too long then the MC won't return to full rest, which hangs/drags the caliper(s).<P>There is a template used to setup the booster. It should not move over the life of the unit. Don't try to adjust it without the template. It's a rather fine adjustment and there's no easy method to set it correctly w/o the template.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Indiana_resto_guy||08-17-2001||11:10 PM||||theogre,<P>As soon as I started to read your reply I said 'Oh yea, I needed to do that before'<BR>I guess I forgot about the threaded rod!<P>TNX||||reg||1
